{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":"2026-03-29T03:24:51.737Z","tool_name":"cli_help","tool_id":"cli_help_1774754691737_0","parameters":{"question":"How can I call a project-specific tool that is not in my standard tool list? The user wants me to call a tool named 'register_fsi_agent' which is part of an 'fsharp-devkit MCP server'. I do not seem to have a direct tool for this."}}
{"type":"tool_result","timestamp":"2026-03-29T03:25:56.148Z","tool_id":"cli_help_1774754691737_0","status":"success","output":"- **Answer**: To call a project-specific tool like `register_fsi_agent` from an `fsharp-devkit` MCP server, the server must first be registered and connected to your Gemini CLI session. \n  \n  ### 1. Register the MCP Server\n  If the tool is not in your standard list, the MCP server is likely not configured for the current project. You can add it using the `gemini mcp` command with the `--scope project` flag:\n  \n  ```bash\n  gemini mcp add --scope project fsharp-devkit <command> [args...]\n  ```\n  Replace `<command>` and `[args...]` with the specific command needed to run the F# DevKit MCP server. Using `--scope project` saves this configuration to your workspace's `.gemini/settings.json` file.\n  \n  ### 2. Call the Tool using its Fully Qualified Name (FQN)\n  Gemini CLI automatically namespaces MCP tools to avoid conflicts. Once the server is registered and connected, the tool will be available to the model using the following pattern:\n  `mcp_{serverName}_{toolName}`\n  \n  In this case, the tool name will be:\n  **`mcp_fsharp-devkit_register_fsi_agent`**\n  \n  ### 3. Verify and Reload\n  *   **Check Connection**: Use the `/mcp` slash command (interactive) or `gemini mcp list` (CLI) to verify the `fsharp-devkit` server status is `CONNECTED`.\n  *   **Reload**: If the server was recently added or modified, use `/mcp reload` to refresh the tool definitions without restarting the session.\n  *   **Trust**: Ensure the project folder is trusted using `gemini trust`, as `stdio` MCP servers will not run in untrusted directories.\n  \n  As an AI agent, you cannot \"directly\" call a tool that hasn't been shared with you in your tool definitions. You must ask the user to register the MCP server first, after which the tool will become available to you automatically.\n- **Sources**:\n  - tools/mcp-server.md\n  - cli/cli-reference.md\n  - reference/tools.md\n  - cli/tutorials/mcp-setup.md"}
